<div class="extension-cord-usage-section" id="extension-cord-usage">
  <div class="page-width">
    <h2 class="big-title">
      Extension Cord Usage:
      <strong>Best Practices & Safety Tips</strong>
    </h2>
    <div class="content-holder">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <h2>Understanding Gauge in Extension Cords</h2>
        <p>
          The gauge (AWG – American Wire Gauge) of an extension cord refers to the thickness of the wire inside the
          cord. The lower the gauge number, the thicker the wire, which means it can handle more electrical current
          (amps) and deliver power more efficiently.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-responsive">
        <table class="table">
          <thead>
            <th>Gauge (AWG)</th>
            <th>Usage</th>
            <th>Max Amps</th>
            <th>Typical Applications</th>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th>AWG 16</th>
              <td><a class="btn" href="#">Light-Duty</a></td>
              <td>2-prong, ungrounded</td>
              <td>Lamps, chargers, small appliances</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th>AWG 14</th>
              <td><a class="btn" href="#">Medium-Duty</a></td>
              <td>3-prong, grounded (most common household outlet)</td>
              <td>Computers, TVs, kitchen appliances</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th>AWG 12</th>
              <td><a class="btn" href="#">Heavy-Duty</a></td>
              <td>Similar to 5-15 but with one horizontal prong</td>
              <td>Power tools, heavier appliances</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th>AWG 10</th>
              <td><a class="btn" href="#">Extra Heavy-Duty</a></td>
              <td>250V plugs for high-power devices</td>
              <td>Air conditioners, industrial tools</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="extension-cord-columns" id="key-factors">
  <div class="page-width">
    <div class="st-row custom-st-row">
      <div class="st-col-md-6 column">
        <div class="column__box gutters-end-0">
          <h2 class="title">
            Key Factors to Consider When Choosing an
            <strong>Extension Cord Gauge</strong>
          </h2>
          <div class="subtitle-filled">POWER NEEDS</div>
          <div class="description gutters-end-0">
            <p>
              Always match the gauge to the power consumption (wattage/amps) of your device. High-wattage appliances
              such as heaters, air conditioners, and power tools require
              <strong>12 AWG or lower</strong> to safely carry the current load.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="st-col-md-6 column">
        <div class="column__box gutters-end-0">
          <h2 class="title">
            Extension Cords: Short vs. Long Cords and Choosing
            <strong>the Right AWG</strong>
          </h2>
          <div class="description gutters-end-0">
            <p>
              The length of an extension cord affects how efficiently it delivers power. Longer cords experience more
              resistance, which can cause voltage drop and overheating if the gauge (AWG) is too small.
              <strong>Selecting the correct wire gauge</strong> ensures safe and efficient power delivery
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

